{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nsources=[dict(id='S_node_advance_order',file='node.go',start_line=158,end_line=187,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S_raw_advance_contract',file='rawnode.go',start_line=477,end_line=494,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S_pending_read_purpose',file='raft.go',start_line=428,end_line=432,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S_read_api_contract',file='node.go',start_line=218,end_line=229,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S_read_raw_entry',file='rawnode.go',start_line=556,end_line=563,kind='code_observation'),dict(id='S_new_leader_read_test',file='raft_test.go',start_line=2046,end_line=2112,kind='test_expectation')]\ns=dict(action='explore',question='Under a synchronous Ready/Advance caller that persists a committed configuration but delays ApplyConfChange until after Advance, can actual election and configuration activation cause the first current-term commit while pre-commit ReadIndex requests remain queued? Observe the queue and public Ready.ReadStates through heartbeats, a fresh read, and a later proposal. The legality of delaying configuration application under the documented early-Advance optimization remains a separate contract question.',rationale='The accepted review handoff selected configuration-driven commit versus pending-read release. Source provides both a blocking campaign guard and an early-Advance interface optimization, so a source-only assertion that the missing release is unreachable would be premature. This is construction/reachability exploration, not a formal property comparison or finding.',sources=sources,harness_path='read_config_explore_test.go',files={},feedback=dict(ref_ids=['S_node_advance_order','S_raw_advance_contract','S_pending_read_purpose','S_read_api_contract','S_transition','S_membership','S_responses','S_read_release','S_new_leader_read_test'],answered='Read the actual pendingReadIndexMessages writers/consumer, configuration commit path and election guard. The only explicit pending-read release is after acknowledgment-driven maybeCommit; switchToConfig can commit but does not call that release. A regular not-yet-advanced committed configuration prevents campaigning via hasUnappliedConfChanges. However Node.Advance explicitly permits advancing while application is in progress, while RawNode.Advance describes applied-and-saved completion and ApplyConfChange is required whenever a configuration is observed. This is a real ordering/applicability gap, not an established violation. ReadIndex also permits request loss and caller retry, so generic eventual response cannot be assumed. Built a four-node exploration with actual bootstrap, configuration proposal, early Advance, election, queued read and delayed configuration activation; local execution only guided construction.',remaining=['Trusted clean-copy exploration output must establish the actual prefix and observed endpoints.','Resolve whether the early-Advance optimization permits deferring ApplyConfChange specifically, rather than only slower application work after membership has been updated.','If that ordering is permitted, determine whether the internal queue-handling expectation supports a narrow implementation contract despite the public lossy ReadIndex contract; otherwise explain the observed conditional history as outside the valid caller order.'],understanding='deferred',rationale='Preserve the completed stale-completion result without changes. Compare (1) this concrete configuration/read interaction with known alternate campaign protection and a precise API-ordering unknown, (2) learner versus voter read quorum eligibility with explicit filtering, and (3) full delayed-storage overwrite history with longer construction. Select (1) for exploration because it can distinguish a reachable alternate commit path from an invalid caller prefix before spending an obligation. No map change or Candidate is asserted from local observations.'))\njson.dump(s,open('config-read-explore.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess\nr=json.load(open('../research.json'));p=subprocess.run(r['validation']['command']+['config-read-explore.json'],capture_output=True,text=True);print(p.stdout);print(p.stderr)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
